## 5. Architectural Layers

### 5.1 Hardware

Aurora ultimately executes on physical hardware.

Examples include:

- CPU;
- GPU;
- RAM;
- storage;
- network adapters;
- audio hardware;
- display hardware;
- input devices;
- cameras;
- microphones.

Aurora should avoid directly managing hardware when an established Linux subsystem already provides the required functionality.

---

## 5.2 Linux Kernel

The Linux kernel forms the lowest software layer of Aurora.

Aurora relies on the kernel for:

- process scheduling;
- memory management;
- hardware drivers;
- filesystems;
- networking;
- device access;
- process isolation;
- namespaces;
- cgroups;
- security mechanisms;
- system calls.

Aurora-specific software should remain primarily outside the kernel.

### Kernel modification policy

Kernel modifications should be considered only when absolutely necessary.

The preferred order is:

1. Existing Linux API
2. Existing userspace subsystem
3. Existing kernel configuration
4. Existing kernel module
5. Aurora userspace solution
6. Kernel modification as a last resort

This keeps Aurora maintainable across future Linux kernel versions.

---

## 5.3 Linux Userspace

Linux userspace provides the foundation upon which Aurora services operate.

Important components may include:

- `systemd` for service management;
- D-Bus for system IPC;
- Wayland for graphical display;
- PipeWire for multimedia;
- standard Linux libraries;
- networking services;
- filesystem infrastructure;
- package-management infrastructure.

Aurora should integrate with these systems rather than unnecessarily replacing them.

---

## 6. Aurora System Services

Aurora-specific functionality lives primarily in userspace services.

Potential services include:

```
aurora-ai
aurora-context
aurora-search
aurora-settings
aurora-update
aurora-notification

```

Not all of these need to exist in the MVP.

The architecture deliberately allows services to be added independently.

---

## 7. Aurora AI Service

The **Aurora AI Service** is the central AI integration point of the operating system.

Its purpose is to provide controlled AI capabilities to authorized applications and system components.

It should not itself be responsible for every AI-related task.

Instead, it acts as an orchestrator and policy boundary.

---

### 7.1 Responsibilities

The Aurora AI Service may eventually be responsible for:

- accepting AI requests;
- authenticating callers;
- enforcing permissions;
- selecting an AI provider;
- managing model lifecycle;
- managing inference requests;
- streaming responses;
- managing AI context;
- applying privacy policies;
- tracking resource usage;
- exposing model capabilities;
- handling failures;
- terminating unhealthy model workers.

It should **not** directly implement every AI feature.

---

