## 41. Configuration

Aurora configuration should be separated into logical categories.

```
System Configuration
       │
       ├── Hardware
       ├── Services
       └── Security

User Configuration
       │
       ├── Preferences
       ├── AI settings
       └── Applications

AI Configuration
       │
       ├── Provider
       ├── Model
       └── Resource policy

```

Applications should not directly modify configuration belonging to other components.

---

## 42. Updates

Aurora should rely heavily on the underlying Linux distribution's mature update infrastructure initially.

Aurora-specific components should have a controlled update mechanism.

Eventually:

```
Update Source
      │
      ▼
Signature Verification
      │
      ▼
Package Validation
      │
      ▼
Installation
      │
      ▼
Health Check

```

Updates should be authenticated and integrity-protected.

---

## 43. Compatibility Strategy

Aurora should aim for compatibility at multiple levels.

### Kernel compatibility

Use standard Linux interfaces.

### Userspace compatibility

Use standard Linux libraries and conventions.

### Application compatibility

Avoid unnecessarily changing existing application interfaces.

### AI compatibility

Provide a stable Aurora AI API independent of the model implementation.

This produces:

```
Stable Aurora Interface
          │
          ├── Model A
          ├── Model B
          ├── Model C
          └── Cloud Provider

```

---

## 44. Testing Architecture

Aurora should be tested at several levels.

### 44.1 Unit Tests

Test individual components.

Examples:

- permission evaluation;
- request validation;
- model selection;
- configuration parsing;
- API serialization.

---

### 44.2 Integration Tests

Test interactions between components.

Examples:

```
Application
    ↓
D-Bus
    ↓
Aurora AI
    ↓
Mock Provider
    ↓
Response

```

---

### 44.3 Security Tests

Test:

- unauthorized IPC calls;
- privilege escalation attempts;
- malformed requests;
- malicious application behavior;
- invalid tool requests;
- sandbox escapes;
- prompt-injection-related tool abuse.

---

### 44.4 Failure Tests

Aurora should deliberately test failures.

Examples:

- kill AI worker;
- disconnect network;
- remove model;
- exhaust available memory;
- terminate system services;
- corrupt configuration;
- deny permissions.

The expected result should be graceful degradation rather than system-wide failure.

